 so you've got  growing antibiotic resistance which is  not a good thing and then we have  another problem and that's the fact that  the rate of anti pie  discovery is decreasing if you look at  the number of antibiotics that have been  approved for use in humans over the last  several decades the number being  approved over time is going down some  people call this you know the age of  resistance the best the the age that  we're entering I've heard it referred to  many times as the next big health crisis  the World Health Organization has  actually referred to this trend of  antibiotic resistance as quote one of  the biggest threats to global health  security and development today so we do  need new approaches we need new  approaches to discover more antibiotics  to fight these superbugs these pan  resistant bacteria all of these types of  infections that are gaining antibiotic  resistance all the time there's one that  I've been aware of for a few years now  called the I check I'll drop a link to  an article about it in the video  description below but the I chip is this  kind of cool contraption where bacteria  from like the soil or marshes they would  not typically grow on agar plates in a  laboratory can be grown in this kind of  this I chipped technology it's basically  where it has all of these little holes  so you dilute the bacteria sample down  to there's just one bacterium per this  little basically microscopic hole and it  has like membranes around it so that  compounds can get in and out so you can  stick it down in its soil or in its  marshy environment but be able to study  those individual bacteria that would not  grow on just like a regular nutrient  agar plate and so there are antibiotics  that have been discovered that weight  EXO back ten is one that comes to mind  but the main focus of today's video  which I'm finally getting around to is a  BBC news article from February 21st  it's called scientists discover powerful  antibiotic using AI so AI 